From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] powerhint: keep the GPU at or below 550MHz at all times

ALTERNATIVE to "powerhint: keep the GPU at or below 550MHz during LAUNCH"
(patches/gts2-common/0009 of the combined set). Use one or the other;
both are numbered 0009 and apply on top of 0002-0008.

To use it (from the project root; the two files have different names,
so the other one has to be deleted, and prepare-tree.sh stops if both
are present):
  rm patches/gts2-common/0009-powerhint-keep-the-GPU-at-or-below-550MHz-during-LAUNCH.patch
  cp staging/gpu-permanent-cap/patches/gts2-common/0009-*.patch patches/gts2-common/
If the tree already has the LAUNCH patch applied, revert it first, before
the rm (and the same the other way round when switching back):
  git -C lineage/device/samsung/gts2-common apply -R \
      "$PWD/patches/gts2-common/0009-powerhint-keep-the-GPU-at-or-below-550MHz-during-LAUNCH.patch"

The Mali platform code ties a big-cluster maximum to the GPU clock
(gpu_exynos5433.c, last column of gpu_dvfs_table_default: 1300000 at 700
and 600MHz, 1800000 at 550, 500 and 420MHz, none below) and posts it as a
PM QoS request whenever it sets the GPU clock (gpu_pmqos.c). At 600MHz or
more the big cores are therefore held at 1.3GHz. The LAUNCH-only patch
lifts that for the five seconds of an app launch. Measurements of
2026-11-06 (logs/gpu-cap-scroll-20261106-191308/30-analysis.txt) show
that the same cap applied permanently is never worse for scrolling and
animations and slightly better wherever the power HAL held a 600MHz
minimum (EXPENSIVE_RENDERING). The owner does not play games on this
tablet, which is what the 600 and 700MHz steps would be for.

What changes, in the power HAL configuration only (no kernel change):

- GPUMaxFreq (dvfs_max_lock): the default becomes "550" and no action
  uses the node any more. With ResetOnInit the HAL writes 550 once when
  it starts. The GPUMaxFreq actions of SUSTAINED_PERFORMANCE (600) and
  FIXED_PERFORMANCE (700) are removed, and LAUNCH needs none.
- GPUMinFreq (dvfs_min_lock): "600" is replaced by "550", and
  EXPENSIVE_RENDERING (was 600) and FIXED_PERFORMANCE (was 700, which the
  kernel clamped to 600) ask for 550, so the minimum is never above the
  maximum. The resting minimum stays at 500 (default, last index):
  releasing it made scrolling less smooth (measured the same day).
- The "Comments" of both nodes describe this, including the limits below.

Checked in the kernel source: 550 is a row of the DVFS table, which both
store handlers require (set_max_lock_dvfs/set_min_lock_dvfs in
gpu_custom_interface.c reject a clock that gpu_dvfs_get_level() does not
find between the minimum and maximum clock); the minimum handler also
clamps to gpu_max_clock_limit, 600 here, so 550 passes unchanged; and
gpu_check_target_clock() applies the minimum lock first and the maximum
lock last. The 550 row has cpu_max_freq 1800000, the 600 row 1300000.
gpu_dvfs_decide_max_clock() can lower the maximum clock of a chip by its
table version; a chip limited below 550 would reject the write. This
tablet accepts it: the measurements above were made with the cap set.

Two known limits:
- From kernel boot until the power HAL starts the GPU is not capped.
- The HAL writes the value once. A later write to dvfs_max_lock by
  something else is not corrected until the HAL restarts.

Deviation from the plain form of this change: GPUMaxFreq is not reduced
to the single value "550". Its "0" and "700" entries, and "0" and "700"
of GPUMinFreq, stay in the lists although no action uses them (the
default index of GPUMaxFreq is 2). They are the three context lines of
the "Path" to "Paths" hunks of patch 0002. With them gone, "git apply
--reverse --check" of 0002 fails on a tree that has both patches, and
scripts/prepare-tree.sh, which uses that check to skip patches that are
already applied, stops on its second run (tested: the single-value form
makes 0002 fail that check, this form does not). The HAL behaves the
same either way: it writes the default once and nothing can request the
unused values. The single-value form is possible only folded into 0002.

libperfmgr drops every action when one names a value its node does not
have (HintManager::ParseActions clears the result and the HAL gets no
configuration), which is why the two GPUMaxFreq actions are removed in
the same patch that takes "600" out of the lists.

Validated: JSON; the rules of HintManager::ParseNodes/ParseActions
(11 nodes, 49 actions, every action value defined in its node, one
action per node and hint); applies after 0002-0008 and leaves every
gts2-common patch reverse-applicable. Not built, not run on the device.
---
 configs/powerhint.json | 39 +++++++++++++++------------------------
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)

